@DISTRO_ID@-session (0.1.5) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Depend on usbmuxd, the service through which Linux talks to iPhones and
    iPads: libimobiledevice only recommends it, so with Recommends off gvfs's
    Apple device monitor (gvfs-afc-volume-monitor) could not reach them and
    Files could not browse them (docs/7 choice 46).
  * New user unit @DISTRO_ID@-volume-monitors.service, wanted by
    graphical-session.target: runs "gio mount -l" right after login, so the
    gvfs volume monitors start in the background. The camera monitor took 2
    to 4 s with an iPad connected, and Files' first launch of a session
    waited for it (4.9 s on the Legion laptop, docs/7 choice 46).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-session (0.1.4) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Depend on intel-media-va-driver-non-free (multiverse), Intel's VA-API
    driver, so every machine with an Intel GPU decodes video in hardware;
    Mesa provides VA-API for AMD (mesa-va-drivers) but not for Intel. It
    came only with @DISTRO_ID@-hw-legion before (docs/7 choice 44).
  * The GPU environment generator uses /etc/@DISTRO_ID@/gpu only when a GPU
    it names drives the display the firmware booted on (boot_vga), so a
    laptop switched to its discrete GPU in the firmware (MUX) does not get
    Hyprland limited to a GPU without a screen (docs/7 choice 45).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 21:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-session (0.1.3) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * The GPU environment generator recognizes an AMD GPU beside an Intel or
    AMD display GPU: it exports @DISTRO_ID_UPPER@_GPU_OFFLOAD=amd and the AMD
    GPU's PCI address in Mesa's DRI_PRIME form
    (@DISTRO_ID_UPPER@_GPU_OFFLOAD_DEVICE), for @DISTRO_ID@-launch-dgpu and the
    menu (docs/7 choice 43). The PCI scan reads @DISTRO_ID_UPPER@_GPU_SYSFS when
    set, for tests/gpu-env.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-session (0.1.2) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * The GPU environment generator finds the GPUs from the PCI devices of the
    display class rather than /sys/class/drm, so hybrid graphics is detected
    before the NVIDIA driver has loaded (docs/7 choice 40).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-session (0.1.1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Add a udev rule giving the "video" group write access to the display and
    keyboard backlights: brightnessctl, which the port's brightness commands
    call, writes sysfs directly, and Ubuntu's package ships no rule and no
    logind support, so brightness could not be changed (Legion laptop,
    2026-10-11). The installer's users.conf and the live session put the user
    in "video".
  * Add a user environment generator, 50-@DISTRO_ID@-gpu, that exports
    AQ_DRM_DEVICES to Hyprland from the kernel command line (@DISTRO_ID@.gpu=),
    from /etc/@DISTRO_ID@/gpu (hardware packages) or, in the live session on a
    dual-GPU machine, the boot GPU; by-path names are resolved to the card
    node, since aquamarine fails to start when given the symlink.
  * On hybrid graphics (Intel or AMD driving the display, an NVIDIA GPU
    beside it) the generator points applications' EGL and Vulkan at Mesa:
    starting an application woke the NVIDIA GPU and took 3.9 s instead of
    0.33 s (Legion laptop, 2026-10-12). oaxaca-launch-nvidia runs one on
    the NVIDIA GPU.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 16:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-session (0.1.0) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: graphics and session metapackage (Mesa, Hyprland,
    Xwayland, uwsm, SDDM, portals, GNOME Keyring, Quickshell >= 0.3.2) and
    SDDM's Wayland greeter configuration.

